[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-42", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 42 — keyed clause shapes straight to the index; IS3 -9%, the ceiling unchanged", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-42.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 42 - the keyed clause shapes go straight to the index; the ceiling does not move\n\n> Seed: iteration 41's S33. Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2; rung :sf1-full;\n> SUT levi (M4 4P+6E, 16 threads), driver judah, LAN.\n\n## Generate / Rank\n\n  The general keyed path substitutes each binding into a pattern, groups\n  equal patterns, scans each into a vector of quad maps and unifies each\n  row back -- four allocations a row before the set. The two shapes that\n  cover most LDBC clauses now extend each binding from one index lookup:\n  [?e a ?v] with ?e bound in every binding (:eavt), and [?e a v] with v\n  bound or ground and ?e free (:avet). visible? still sees every quad as\n  a map; a pattern source keeps the general path. Tests: hand-written\n  expected sets for both shapes, wildcard and ground values, a hidden\n  quad, and four declining controls (neither side bound, entity not\n  bound in every binding, attribute not ground, no bindings).\n\n  At saturation (1 slot, back-to-back): IS3 5.8 -> 5.3 ms, IC07 4.0 ->\n  3.7, IC09 5.1 -> 5.0. IS2 and IC08 read +0.2 ms -- inside restart-to-\n  restart variance for sub-millisecond ops (iteration 41 saw IC08 move\n  0.68 -> 0.40 across two restarts with no relevant change); an A/B in\n  one JVM would be needed to see it, and was not done.\n\n    rung sf1-full  slots  threads  reads/s  upd/s  on-time%  thru/s\n      it.41          64     16      3328     1475   100.00    4797.0\n      it.42          64     16      3328     1475   100.00    4785.6   (3712: 65.1% fails)\n\n  Unchanged. Tests: datalog JVM 138/476/0, cljs 142/487/0; kotobase-peer\n  263/808/0.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - The per-op wins are real and small; the ceiling is set by the\n    host's total CPU under the whole mix, and the keyed path was ~1 us\n    of a 1.2 ms mean op. Three iterations of constant-factor work on\n    the engine (41, 42) have bought +10%; the next factor of 2 is not\n    in the join's allocations.\n  - IS3 is still 7x its hand-written twin (5.3 vs 0.72 ms): what is\n    left is the binding maps themselves, hashed into a set at every\n    clause -- a representation change, not a hot-path patch (S34).\n  - M4 is the wall in plain numbers now: 4 performance cores against\n    the audited No.1's 160. The road from here is a host, and the SF3\n    rung (~13 GB) needs one too.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: no row moved (4797.0 stands);\nengine change landed on per-op evidence. Ratio to the audited No.1: 29.\n\nSeeds for iteration 43, in order:\n\n  - M4: a host with performance cores for the SUT (and memory for SF3).\n  - S26: a full-data parameter set. S23b: IC05.\n  - S34: bindings as arrays inside the join (the 7x to the twin).\n"}]
